+## pararules 1.4.0 `pararules.nim` (the macros), patched for paraloop (see
+## pararules.diff for the exact changes against upstream). Two changes, both
+## for the compile cost of a large ruleset:
+##
+## 1. `schema` (`createSessionProcs`) emits ONE generic `insert` and ONE
+##    generic `retract` (plus the id-and-attr `retract` and `contains`)
+##    instead of an `insert` and a `retract` overload per value type. Upstream
+##    gives every overload the parameter `session: var Session[Fact, auto]`,
+##    and for a parameter of that shape Nim instantiates the whole `Session`
+##    type afresh, uncached, for every candidate at every call
+##    (`compiler/sigmatch.nim`, the `tyGenericInvocation` branch that calls
+##    `prepareMetatypeForSigmatch`). With one game's 42 value types that
+##    was 43 candidates and 60-120 ms of compile time per
+##    `session.insert(...)`/`session.retract(...)` line: about 87 % of the
+##    semantic-analysis time of the game's closure, and the instantiated types
+##    are never freed (5.6 GB of compiler memory). The generic procs take a
+##    bare `Session`, which does not take that path, and overload resolution
+##    has one candidate to try.
+##
+## 2. `staticRuleset` (`createTypesForSession` and the three
+##    `createCaseOfKey*` procs) makes the match type `kind` plus ONE array of
+##    slots, `vals: array[N, tuple[fact: Fact, isSet: bool]]` with N the
+##    variable count of the widest rule, instead of an object variant with a
+##    branch per rule holding a named slot per variable. The generated `[]`,
+##    `[]=` and `hasKey` still switch on `kind` and then on the variable's
+##    name; they now reach slot i (the variable's position in its rule) where
+##    they reached the field of that name. With a branch per rule, every
+##    lifetime hook (`=copy`, `=destroy`, `=dup`, `=sink`) of every type that
+##    holds a match expanded every branch inline, and each slot is itself the
+##    whole `Fact` variant: 78 of the 92 MB of C generated for the closure of
+##    that game's 121 rules, and most of what the C compiler was given.
+##
+## Change 1 changes nothing at run time. The generic `insert`/`retract` wrap
+## the value with the same `init<Fact>` overload set the per-type procs called,
+## run the same debug-build type check (`check<Fact>`) and call the same
+## `insertFact`/`retractFact`; the generated C is the same apart from names and
+## source paths. Two things differ at compile time:
+##
+## - A value whose type is not in the schema fails inside the generic proc
+##   (no `init<Fact>` overload matches) instead of at the call.
+## - The bare `Session` parameter means two schemas in one program must not
+##   share an attribute enum, or their `insert`s are ambiguous. Every schema
+##   here declares its own.
+##
+## Change 2 changes the match type's layout and nothing a rule or a caller can
+## see: the engine reads a match only through `[]`, `[]=`, `hasKey` and the
+## `initMatch` constructor, and every query, `then` and hash gives what it
+## gave. The object is the same size (a variant is as big as its widest
+## branch). What differs:
+##
+## - A match's hooks walk all N slots where the variant's walked the active
+##   rule's own, so copying a match of a narrow rule does a little more work
+##   (an unset slot is a default `Fact`, with nothing to copy or free).
+##   Measured at that game's mob cap (`para sim`, seed 7, 21600 ticks, five
+##   runs a side): a debug build's tick went from 1.87 to 2.06 ms p50 and
+##   from 7.42 to 7.63 ms p99; a release build's did not slow (0.92 to 0.86
+##   ms p99). A rule far wider than the rest widens every match, as before.
+## - The match type has no field named after a rule any more. Nothing here
+##   read one; `match.vals` is the only field besides `kind`.
+## - The per-rule tuple types (`createTupleType`, `ruleNameToTupleType`) are
+##   still built and passed, unused, so the diff stays small.
+##
+## The procs upstream used to build the per-type overloads (`createUpdateProc`,
+## `createInsertProc`, `createRetractProc`, `createContainsProc`) are left in
+## place, unused, so the diff against upstream stays small.
+##
+## Bumping pararules: re-apply pararules.diff to the new version's
+## `pararules.nim`, keep `paraloopSchemaPatched`, and regenerate the diff
+## (`diff -u --label "pararules.nim (1.4.0)" --label "pararules.nim (patched)"
+## <upstream>/pararules.nim patches/pararules/pararules.nim`).
+## tests/test_schema_patch.nim fails if either change is missing.
+
 import pararules/engine, tables, sets, macros, strutils
 
+const paraloopSchemaPatched* = true
